git commit: 13282a8bd06bd33324a4dbfd3c1c03685f3cd9aa

The mysql client was invoked with explicit -h 127.0.0.1 -P 14000. The
tidbbug login path points to a different TiDB and was not used for this run.

Setup:
  CREATE DATABASE ai_native_txn_advisor_live_20260712;
  CREATE TABLE t_direct(id INT PRIMARY KEY, v INT);
  CREATE TABLE t_red(id INT PRIMARY KEY, v INT);
  CREATE TABLE t_control(id INT PRIMARY KEY, v INT);
  INSERT INTO each table VALUES (1,10);
  stale_ts = 2026-07-12 12:58:03.379233
  wait 2 seconds
  INSERT INTO each table VALUES (2,20);

Current-rowset control:
  SELECT id,v FROM t_red ORDER BY id;
  -> 1 10
  -> 2 20

Direct stale-read control, in a fresh connection:
  SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY AS OF TIMESTAMP '2026-07-12 12:58:03.379233';
  SELECT 'DIRECT_STALE_READ', id, v FROM t_direct ORDER BY id;
  -> DIRECT_STALE_READ  1  10

RED, in a fresh connection:
  SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY AS OF TIMESTAMP '2026-07-12 12:58:03.379233';
  RECOMMEND INDEX RUN FOR
    'SELECT * FROM ai_native_txn_advisor_live_20260712.t_red WHERE id >= 1';
  SELECT 'AFTER_ADVISOR', id, v FROM t_red ORDER BY id;
  -> AFTER_ADVISOR  1  10
  -> AFTER_ADVISOR  2  20

No-pending-state control, in a fresh connection:
  RECOMMEND INDEX RUN FOR
    'SELECT * FROM ai_native_txn_advisor_live_20260712.t_control WHERE id >= 1';
  SELECT 'CONTROL_NO_PENDING_STATE', id, v FROM t_control ORDER BY id;
  -> CONTROL_NO_PENDING_STATE  1  10
  -> CONTROL_NO_PENDING_STATE  2  20

Interpretation:
  The direct AS OF oracle proves that the timestamp falls between the two
  committed row versions. RECOMMEND INDEX RUN itself succeeds, but its
  current-session ExecuteInternal helper SQL consumes the pending one-shot
  TxnReadTS. The following user SELECT therefore reads the current rowset.
  The no-pending control rules out a general failure of RECOMMEND INDEX RUN.

Source anchors:
  pkg/executor/recommend_index.go:75
  pkg/planner/indexadvisor/utils.go:533-549
  pkg/session/session.go:1875-1901
  pkg/sessiontxn/staleread/processor.go:233-257

Contract gate:
  docs/design/2021-09-22-stale-read.md:68-80 and :100-112 describe SET
  TRANSACTION stale read as applying to the next interactive transaction or
  query statement. Final product escalation still requires the owner to confirm
  that helper SQL executed inside a user management statement is not itself the
  user's intended next query. The live RED is nevertheless user-visible and
  independent of the internal TxnReadTS counter.
